      "writtenOn": "2026-06-05T01:46:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The \"Missing property Name/Polarity\" messages are produced by the current probing logic in CPLDSoftwareManager::initDevice().\n\nThe loop checks MuxOutputs0, MuxOutputs1, MuxOutputs2, ... until it reaches an entry that does not exist:\n```\n    for (size_t i \u003d 0; true; i++)\n    {\n        const std::string iface \u003d configIfaceMux + std::to_string(i);\n\n        std::optional\u003cstd::string\u003e name \u003d\n            co_await dbusGetRequiredProperty\u003cstd::string\u003e(..., iface, \"Name\");\n\n        std::optional\u003cstd::string\u003e polarity \u003d\n            co_await dbusGetRequiredProperty\u003cstd::string\u003e(..., iface, \"Polarity\");\n\n        if (!name.has_value() || !polarity.has_value())\n        {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        names.push_back(name.value());\n        values.push_back((polarity \u003d\u003d \"High\") ? 1 : 0);\n    }\n```\ndbusGetRequiredProperty() logs an error when the property is missing. So when the loop probes the first non-existent MuxOutputsN, it logs the missing Name/Polarity message and then exits the loop.\n\nIn this case, Polarity is defined for the existing MuxOutputs entry. The log comes from probing the next non-existent MuxOutputs entry, not from the existing configuration.\n\nAlso, we confirmed your observation that this approach does not address the GPIO failure itself. We still reproduced the GPIO failure while testing it on our machine.",
